    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `extract_invoice` in `app/invoices.py` using structured outputs. I haven't sent it to the real API. I did run it against a mocked HTTP layer, where it built the request correctly and decoded a sample reply into the expected dict.\n\n**How it works:**\n- It sends one `client.beta.messages.create` call with `MODEL`, `max_tokens=1024` and `betas=[FILES_BETA]`. The invoice goes in as `invoice_block(file_id)`, followed by a short instruction to extract the fields.\n- `output_config={\"format\": {\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"schema\": INVOICE_SCHEMA}}` makes the API constrain the reply text to the schema. The prompt doesn't ask for JSON and no tool call is involved. I checked that SDK 1.8.0 accepts this parameter on the beta endpoint and that this is the current form (the old `output_format=` is gone).\n- It joins the text blocks of the reply and returns them decoded with `json.loads`. Only text blocks are read because this model always thinks, so the reply also contains thinking blocks.\n- **One addition you didn't ask for:** if `stop_reason` isn't `end_turn`, it raises a `RuntimeError` instead of decoding. A refusal, or hitting the 1,024-token limit, can leave the text incomplete, and this stops a bad row from reaching the ledger import.\n\n**Testing:** `pytest -q` passes, but neither of the two existing tests covers `extract_invoice`. For the mocked check, I confirmed the request had the right beta header, model, `max_tokens`, `output_config` and document block, and that a reply with a thinking block plus a JSON text block came back as the right dict.\n\nThe check script `_check_extract.py` is still in the project root because deleting it needed your approval, so please delete it or turn it into a test."
  },
